{[%evp 0,23,25,42,24,17,28,3,7,0,7,-57,-2,-4,5,-41,-12,-61,-47,-36,-42,-42,-26,-20,-23,-19]} 1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 {Welcome to the Vienna, a fresh approach against one of Black's most solid openings. We want to get interesting positions with this line and avoid the mainstream theory.} Nc6 (2... Nf6 {Also an important move, and here we play:} 3. d4 {I highly recommend checking the dedicated chapter to this line, as it's a very important one.}) 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. d3 {This position is the main focus of this database. It can also arise via the 2.Bc4 move order, for example, so it's not only interesting in the Vienna.} Bc5 {[%csl Bc5] The big mainline.} (4... Na5 {In this line Black immediately goes after our bishop.} 5. Bb3 Nxb3 6. axb3 {If Black plays with d6, for example, we can play f4 and get an interesting game. More important for us is:} d5 {The critical line.} 7. exd5 Nxd5 (7... Bb4 {Also possible.} 8. Bg5 Qxd5 9. Nf3 Bg4 {And here we play a very nice novelty:} 10. Ra4 $146 {[%csl Ba4] Already a new and fresh position, most likely leading to an endgame after:} Bxf3 11. Qxf3 Qxf3 12. gxf3 $132 {In the theoretical chapters we will go into more detail, but for now it's more than enough. We've reached an interesting and new position that is very playable.}) 8. Qh5 {This move only has one drawback:} Nb4 9. Qxe5+ {But we can take this pawn.} Be7 10. Kd1 {[%csl Bd1] We defend c2 with the king. It's an interesting position and we have an extra pawn. The king is also not too weak on d1, as we don't have to worry about an immediate attack.} O-O 11. Nf3 Be6 (11... Re8 $6 {Looks very natural, but after:} 12. Qd4 $14 {[%csl Rc8][%cal Rd8c8] Black is more or less forced to exchange queens, and in the endgame the extra pawn definitely grants us an advantage.}) 12. Bd2 Re8 13. Re1 {Is the best now.} (13. Qd4 $6 {Now Black has:} Qc8 $15 {[%csl Bc8] Dodging the queen trade and Black is better.}) 13... c5 {[%csl Bc5][%cal Re5d4] Black should stop Qd4 ideas now.} 14. Ne4 $132 {With a very interesting position to play, and in the theoretical chapters, we will go into detail here of course.}) (4... Bb4 {Also a possible line.} 5. Nge2 d5 {Black's main idea.} 6. exd5 Nxd5 7. O-O Be6 {Most common and best move.} (7... Nxc3 {Also has been played.} 8. bxc3 Bd6 9. Ng3 O-O (9... Na5 {I think it's unlikely someone will play this here and it's also not great.} 10. Bd5 $14 {We can simply secure our bishop and White is slightly better.}) 10. a4 {[%cal Gc4a2] To secure the bishop.} Kh8 11. Ne4 $146 {We use the chance to activate the knight before Black plays f5.} f5 12. Nxd6 cxd6 13. f4 $132 {We have a new and interesting position to play.}) 8. Bxd5 Bxd5 9. f4 f6 $1 {Difficult to play over the board, but it's the best move, so a prepared opponent could play this.} (9... O-O {This has been played in 150/200 games, so there's a 75% chance that your opponent will play this move. It's not bad, but it gives White chances.} 10. Nxd5 {Important to start with this, so that Black has to keep the dark-squared bishop.} (10. f5 {Don't start with this, as it allows:} Bxc3 11. Nxc3 f6 $11 {And Black is just fine, as we don't get a strong knight on e4.}) 10... Qxd5 11. f5 $132 {[%csl Be4,Bf5][%cal Ge2g3,Gg3e4,Gd1g4] We improve the knight to e4 and it's very pleasant to play this for White.}) 10. fxe5 Nxe5 11. Nxd5 Qxd5 12. d4 {Now O-O-O was played in three of six games here but I want to focus on Black's best continuation now:} Ng6 $1 {Played in the other three games and here we don't get much, just a symbolic advantage but it's objectively fully equal.} 13. Nf4 Nxf4 14. Bxf4 O-O-O 15. Qd3 $132 {This is the small idea I have in this position. In the theoretical chapter this position is covered in great detail. But we have a new position and it's enough for now. Our idea here is to meet Bd6 with c4 and get a slightly more comfortable game.}) 5. f4 $5 {[%csl Bf4] Is our idea and now Black has three options:} O-O (5... exf4 {Also logical.} 6. Bxf4 O-O {Best for Black.} (6... d6 {Most common and here we get something very interesting.} 7. Nf3 Bg4 (7... Be6 $6 {Allows:} 8. Bb5 $1 $14 {[%cal Gd3d4] d4 next and White is better.}) 8. Qd2 $1 {[%csl Bf3] Preparing O-O-O and unpinning the knight.} O-O (8... Bxf3 $6 {It's not good for Black to take.} 9. gxf3 Ne5 10. O-O-O $1 {We can just ignore the f3-pawn.} Nh5 (10... Nxf3 $2 {Loses to:} 11. Qg2 $18 {[%csl Rf3,Rg7] g7 is falling if the knight moves.}) 11. Bg5 $16 {White is already much better.}) 9. O-O-O $132 {[%cal Gf4g5,Gh1f1] With a very interesting position to play.}) 7. Nf3 d6 8. d4 $1 {We take the center now.} Bb6 $1 {Most accurate.} (8... Bb4 {Logical, but here we definitely get something.} 9. Bg5 $1 {[%csl Bg5]} h6 $6 {Logical reaction.} (9... Qe8 $1 {Is the best for Black, but we will go into detail in the chapters.}) 10. Bxf6 Qxf6 11. O-O {[%cal Gc3d5]} Bxc3 12. bxc3 Qe7 13. Qe2 $14 {[%cal Ga1e1] We have control over the center and it's slightly better for sure.}) 9. Bg5 {[%csl Bg5] Pinning the knight.} h6 10. Bxf6 Qxf6 11. Nd5 $1 Qg6 $1 {[%csl Re4,Rg2] Attacking the weak pawns.} (11... Qd8 $6 {Logical, but inaccurate.} 12. O-O $16 {[%csl Bd4,Be4][%cal Gc2c3] White is just much better.}) 12. O-O Qxe4 $1 13. c3 $44 {[%csl Bc3,Yd4][%cal Gf3d2,Ge4h4,Gf1f4] We have full compensation and an interesting position to play.}) (5... d6 {Most common continuation.} 6. Nf3 Bg4 (6... a6 {This gives us the option to play:} 7. Nd5 $1 Nxd5 8. Bxd5 Be6 $1 {Important move for Black.} (8... O-O $2 {Positional mistake.} 9. f5 $1 $16 {[%cal Gc1g5,Gg2g4,Rc8e6] White just has a great advantage.}) 9. Bxc6+ $5 {Most interesting option.} bxc6 10. fxe5 dxe5 11. Qe2 $132 {[%cal Gc1e3] We play against the weaker structure now.}) 7. Na4 $1 {[%csl Ba4] Key move.} Bxf3 (7... Bb6 {Most common move.} 8. h3 $1 {We force a reaction now.} Bxf3 9. Qxf3 Nd4 10. Qf2 $1 {There is no strong discovered attack for Black.} O-O 11. fxe5 dxe5 12. c3 Ne6 13. Nxb6 axb6 14. Be3 $16 {With the bishop pair, White has a good advantage.}) (7... O-O {Also logical.} 8. Nxc5 dxc5 9. O-O $36 {We have the bishop pair and a nice position.}) 8. Qxf3 Nd4 9. Qd1 b5 {And now we have a very important line:} 10. Bxf7+ $1 {[%csl Bf7]} Kxf7 11. Nxc5 dxc5 (11... exf4 {Also possible.} 12. Nb3 $16 {But it's clear that White is better now.}) 12. fxe5 Nd7 13. c3 Ne6 14. O-O+ $16 {[%cal Gd3d4] d4 next and we not only have full compensation, but also a great advantage.}) 6. Nf3 d6 {By far the most common and now we play:} (6... Ng4 {[%csl Bg4] Rare but concrete line.} 7. Qe2 $1 {[%cal Rg4f2]} Bf2+ $1 {Critical.} (7... Nf2 $2 {Just doesn't work.} 8. Rf1 Ng4 9. f5 $18 {Black achieved nothing with this knight maneuver.}) (7... d6 $2 {Simply loses.} 8. f5 $18 {[%cal Gc1g5] Bg5 next and Black is busted.}) 8. Kf1 Nd4 9. Nxd4 Bxd4 10. f5 Nf2 $1 $146 {Most critical line.} 11. Qh5 $13 {[%cal Gc1g5] Baadur will show you a lot of interesting lines here in the theoretical chapter.}) 7. f5 $1 {[%cal Cd6d5,Gg2g4,Gc1g5] With a lot of interesting ideas.} Nd4 $1 {[%csl Bd4][%cal Gc7c6,Gd6d5] Black should play for the d5 push now.} (7... h6 {[%csl Rh6][%cal Rc1g5,Gg2g4,Gg4g5] Logical move against Bg5.} 8. a3 {[%csl Ba3,Yc4][%cal Yc4a2] We secure our bishop now.} Ne7 {[%cal Gd6d5]} 9. Qe2 d5 10. Ba2 $40 {[%csl Rg8][%cal Gg2g4] We can slowly castle long and eventually start an attack with g4.}) (7... Na5 {Going after the bishop allows:} 8. Bg5 $1 Nxc4 9. dxc4 c6 $2 10. Qe2 $16 {[%cal Ge1c1] O-O-O next with a big advantage for White.}) 8. Nd5 $1 {[%csl Bd5] We use the last chance to play this move.} (8. Bg5 $2 {Is nothing now.} c6 $15 {No Nd5 ideas and Black is even for choice.}) 8... Nxd5 9. Bxd5 c6 10. Bb3 {[%cal Gf3d4,Gd1h5] We want to take on d4 now, so it makes sense for Black to play:} Nxb3 11. axb3 d5 12. Qe2 $132 {[%cal Gc1e3,Yg2g4,Yg4g5] With a very interesting position for White. Our plan is to exchange the bishops and start advancing the pawns on the kingside.} *

