1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. d3 Bc5 {[%csl Bc5] One of Black's most
important moves. The bishop on c5 aims to transpose into the mainstream
Italian.} 5. f4 $5 {[%csl Bf4] We don't want to transpose into the Italian,
but instead will play in an aggressive yet fully sound style.} ({We don't want
to play} 5. Nf3 {as we would just be transposing into a mainstream Italian.})
5... O-O {This is the topic of this chapter.} (5... d5 {is a concrete move and
will be analyzed separately.}) (5... d6 {is Black's main continuation and will
also be analyzed separately.}) (5... exf4 {will be covered in two separate
chapters.}) 6. Nf3 d6 (6... exf4 {will be analyzed separately.}) (6... Ng4 {
[%csl Bg4] A serious but rare line.} 7. Qe2 $1 {[%cal Rg4f2] A prophylactic
move against Nf2.} (7. Ng5 d5 $1 8. Nxd5 exf4 9. Bxf4 h6 $13) 7... Bf2+ $1 {
The best move, but Black should be well-prepared to play it.} (7... Nf2 $2 {
doesn't make any sense now.} 8. Rf1 Ng4 9. f5 $18 {Black has lost too many
tempi and consequently reached a lost position.}) (7... exf4 8. Bxf4 d6 (8...
Bf2+ $6 {doesn't make sense now.} 9. Kd2 Bb6 10. Bg5 Qe8 11. Nd5 $16) 9. Bg5
Nd4 $1 $146 {Black's best.} (9... Qd7 10. O-O-O Nf2 11. d4 $1 Nxd1 12. Rxd1 Bb6
13. Nd5 $16 {It's not only compensation but an advantage.}) (9... Qe8 10. Nd5
Bf2+ 11. Kd2 Bb6 12. Bb3 Be6 13. d4) 10. Bxd8 Nxe2 {Now we can choose between
two playable endgames:} 11. Bxc7 (11. Kxe2 Rxd8 12. Na4 $14) 11... Nxc3 12.
bxc3 Be6 $1 13. Bxe6 fxe6 14. d4 Ba3 15. Rb1 b6 16. Rb3 Rf7 17. Rxa3 Rxc7 18.
Kd2 $14 {Black has some compensation, but not full.}) (7... d6 $2 {The second
most common move, but a positional mistake.} 8. f5 $18 {[%cal Gc1g5] Bg5 next,
and our position is already winning.}) (7... Nd4 $146 {A risky line but
deserves mention.} 8. Nxd4 exd4 $1 (8... Bxd4 $2 9. f5 $16) 9. Nd5 Nh6 $1 {
A strong engine idea.} (9... d6 10. b4 $1 $16 Bb6 (10... c6 11. bxc5 cxd5 12.
Bxd5 Qa5+ 13. Bd2 Qxc5 14. O-O) 11. O-O c6 12. Nxb6 Qxb6 13. a4 a5 14. bxa5
Rxa5 15. Bd2 Ra8 16. Rab1 Qd8 17. Bb3) 10. f5 (10. b4 $2 Bd6 $17) 10... c6 $1 {
The only move.} 11. O-O (11. f6 $5 {Interesting, but Black equalizes with
strong moves.} cxd5 12. O-O dxc4 13. Qh5 Ng4 $1 14. Qxg4 g6 15. Qg5 Re8 {
[%cal Gc5f8]} 16. Qxc5 d5 $1 $11) 11... cxd5 12. Bxd5 {[%cal Gf5f6] f6 is a
strong threat.} Be7 $1 {The only move against f6.} 13. e5 {[%cal Gf5f6]} (13.
Qh5 $5 {[%cal Re7g5] against Bg5.} Kh8 $1 14. f6 Bxf6 15. e5 Ng8 $1 16. exf6
Nxf6 17. Qf3 Nxd5 18. Qxd5 $36) 13... Bg5 {Necessary.} 14. Qh5 Bxc1 15. Raxc1 {
Black holds with only moves, but the final position is still interesting for
us:} d6 16. f6 Bf5 $1 {[%csl Bf5] The only move.} 17. Rxf5 Nxf5 18. Qxf5 dxe5
19. fxg7 Kxg7 20. Rf1 {[%cal Rd8d5,Gf5f6] Using the fact that Qxd5 is
impossible due to Qf6+.} Qe7 (20... Qxd5 $4 21. Qg5+ Kh8 22. Qf6+ Kg8 23. Rf5
$18 {[%cal Gf5g5]}) 21. Bxb7 $5 Rab8 22. Be4 Rh8 23. b3 $36 {[%csl Be4] A huge
initiative.}) 8. Kf1 Nd4 (8... Bb6 $2 9. h3 $16) 9. Nxd4 Bxd4 {Now White has
many tries.} 10. f5 {I like this approach.} (10. Qxg4 d5 $1 {is Black's idea.}
11. f5 dxc4 12. Nd5 $13 {Balanced and complicated.}) (10. h3 $5 {deserves
attention if you're interested in the arising complications.}) 10... Nf2 $1
$146 {The best move, but there are no games here.} (10... Qh4 $2 11. h3 Nf6 12.
Qf3 $16 {[%csl Rh4][%cal Gg2g3] g3 next. The queen on h4 is misplaced.}) (10...
Nf6 $6 11. g4 $36 {[%cal Gg4g5] A strong attack is coming. The king on f1 is
not a problem.}) 11. Qh5 {[%cal Gc1g5] Preparing Bg5.} c6 $1 {[%cal Gd8b6] An
important only move: Black frees the b6-square for the queen.} (11... Nxh1 $4
12. Bg5 Qe8 13. Nd5 $18) 12. Bg5 Qb6 $1 {[%csl Rb2][%cal Gb6b2] Counterplay.} (
12... Qa5 $2 13. Be7 $18) 13. Na4 Qa5 14. c3 Qxa4 15. cxd4 Nxh1 {A very
interesting position with many possible tries.} 16. f6 {Looks like the most
principled approach.} (16. Be7 $5 {Also interesting, but Black must find many
only moves.} Qc2 $1 ({or} 16... d5 17. f6 Qc2 $1 {[%csl Bc2] The only move,
leading to the same position.}) 17. Kg1 d5 $1 {[%csl Bd5] The only move.} 18.
f6 Qd2 $1 {[%cal Rh5g5,Gd2g5] Holding the g5-square.} (18... Qxb2 $2 19. Rf1
$18) 19. exd5 cxd5 20. Bxd5 Qxb2 21. Rd1 $13 {The evaluation is 0.00 but very
complicated, so it's not just a boring draw.} (21. Rf1 $2 Qxd4+ 22. Kxh1 Qxd3
$15 {[%csl Rf1]})) 16... d5 $1 {[%csl Bd5] The only move. Once again, we have
many tries.} 17. Bh4 (17. Be3 $5 gxf6 $1 18. Qh6 $13) (17. Bd2 gxf6 18. dxe5
Qc2 $1 {[%csl Bc2]} 19. Qh6 fxe5 20. Qg5+ $11 {[%cal Gg5f6,Gf6g5] Perpetual
check follows.}) 17... h6 $3 {[%cal Rh5g5] The only move, preventing Qg5.} 18.
fxg7 Kxg7 19. Kg1 dxc4 20. Bg5 hxg5 21. Qxg5+ $11 {[%cal Gg5h5,Gh5g5] A
perpetual check is coming.}) 7. f5 $1 {[%cal Cd6d5,Gg2g4,Gc1g5] We block the
kingside and are ready to create a quick attack with Bg5-g4. Black's main
counterplay plan is d6-d5, which must be prepared.} Nd4 $1 {[%csl Bd4][%cal
Gc7c6,Gd6d5] Black's best reaction, with the idea of c6-c5.} (7... Ng4 $2 8.
Bg5 $18 {[%cal Rf7f6] The f6 move is not possible.}) (7... a6 $2 {[%cal Gc5a7]
Black secures the a7-square for the bishop but loses an important tempo.} 8.
Bg5 $1 {[%csl Gg5][%cal Gc3d5] Nd5 is a huge threat.} Nb8 {[%cal Gb8d7] A
logical move to defend the f6-knight.} 9. Nd5 Nbd7 10. a3 c6 11. Nxf6+ Nxf6 12.
Qe2 $18 {[%cal Ge1c1,Gg2g4] O-O-O followed by g2-g4, and according to the
engine, White's position is already winning.}) (7... h6 {[%csl Rh6][%cal Rc1g5,
Gg2g4,Gg4g5] Black stops Bg5, but our future g4-g5 plan will be stronger.} 8.
a3 {[%csl Ba3,Yc4][%cal Yc4a2] Securing a square for the bishop. I cannot see
too many plans for Black.} (8. Nd5 $5 {Also fine.} Nxd5 9. Bxd5 Ne7 10. Bb3 d5
11. f6 $1 Ng6 12. fxg7 Kxg7 13. Bxd5 $36) 8... Ne7 {[%cal Gd6d5] Most logical,
preparing d5.} (8... Nd4 9. Nd5 Nxd5 10. Bxd5 c6 11. Ba2 Nxf5 12. exf5 Bxf5 13.
d4 exd4 14. O-O) 9. Qe2 d5 10. Ba2 $40 {[%csl Rg8][%cal Gg2g4] g4 next, with a
huge attack.}) (7... Na5 {Black's most common continuation, but inaccurate!} 8.
Bg5 $1 Nxc4 {This looks less logical than 8...c6 9.a3 Nxc4, but it's Black's
best continuation due to a concrete idea.} (8... c6 {[%cal Rc3d5] Against Nd5
and with the idea of d6-d5.} 9. a3 $1 Nxc4 (9... d5 10. Ba2 $18 {[%cal Gb2b4]})
10. dxc4 $14 {[%cal Gd1d3,Ge1c1] Qd3-O-O-O next, with a huge advantage.}) 9.
dxc4 b5 $1 $146 {An engine improvement. White will be better here too, but at
least Black has counterplay.} (9... c6 $2 10. Qe2 $16 {[%cal Ge1c1]}) 10. cxb5
(10. h4 $5 $40 {[%cal Gf3h2,Gh2g4] Nh2-Ng4 next is also a strong idea.} c6 11.
Nh2 bxc4 12. Ng4) 10... Bb7 11. Qd3 Bb4 $1 {[%csl Re4] Counterplay against e4.}
12. Nd2 a6 $1 13. bxa6 Rxa6 14. O-O $14 {Black has compensation, but not full.}
) 8. Nd5 $1 {[%csl Bd5] Important to play this now, before Black has the
chance to play c7-c6.} (8. Bg5 $2 {The most common and logical move, but here
it's a mistake.} c6 $15 {Black is better, so be careful.}) 8... Nxd5 9. Bxd5 c6
10. Bb3 {[%cal Gf3d4,Gd1h5] We are ready to continue with Nxd4, followed by
Qh5.} Nxb3 {Black should exchange our bishop.} (10... d5 11. Nxd4 Bxd4 12. Qf3
$36) 11. axb3 d5 12. Qe2 $132 {[%cal Gc1e3,Yg2g4,Yg4g5] Be3 next. This is a
playable position. The engine slightly prefers White (+0.20). In the future,
we can start advancing the g-pawn, and it's not easy to see a clear plan for
Black. This is the best Black can achieve in the 6...O-O 7.Nf3 d6 line, so
things look quite promising for us.} *

