Nxd2 13. Bxc7 Nxc4 14. dxc4 Rc8 15. Bxd6 Bxd6 16. Rxd6 Rxc4 17. Rhd1 $16 {[%csl Bd1,Bd6][%cal Gd6d7] We dominate the d-file, which gives us the advantage.}) 9. O-O-O {[%cal Gf4g5,Gh1f1] We are ready to continue with the typical Bg5-Rhf1 idea.} Nd4 {Black's most common move.} (9... Ne5 10. Nxe5 $5 {More ambitious.} (10. Bxe5 dxe5 11. Rhf1 $132 {Very playable.}) 10... dxe5 (10... Bxd1 $4 11. Nxf7 $18) 11. Bxe5 Bxd1 12. Nxd1 Ng4 {Probably Black's most precise continuation.} (12... Bd4 {Sek-Tari, 2019.} 13. Bg3 $36 {Huge initiative for the exchange.}) 13. Bg3 Bd6 14. d4 $5 Bxg3 15. hxg3 $44 {We have full compensation and a very playable position, but objectively it's equal.}) 10. Nxd4 $1 $146 Bxd1 (10... Bxd4 11. Rdf1 $36 {[%cal Gh2h3,Gc3e2,Gf4g5,Gg2g4] A lot of ideas.}) 11. Nf5 $1 Bg4 {And now comes the key move} 12. Nxg7 $3 {[%csl Bg7]} Kxg7 13. Bg5 {[%cal Gh1f1] Rf1 is coming, and Black's position is very dangerous.} Bd4 (13... Be6 14. Rf1 Bd4 {transposes to 13...Bd4.} (14... Ng4 15. Bxd8 Be3 16. Bxc7 Bxd2+ 17. Kxd2 Bxc4 18. dxc4 Rac8 19. Nd5 Rfe8 {[%csl Be8] Only move, otherwise Black's position is lost!} 20. Rf4 Ne5 21. b3 Re6 22. Ba5 $36 {[%cal Ga5c3] Bc3 next, with a strong initiative because our pieces are dominating the board.})) (13... h6 {Engine-style approach, but Black will have to find many difficult moves not to lose immediately:} 14. Bxh6+ Kh7 15. d4 d5 $1 {[%csl Bd5] Only move.} 16. exd5 Ng8 $1 {[%csl Bg8] Only move.} 17. Bd3+ f5 18. Bxf8 Bxf8 19. Kb1 $36 {[%cal Gh2h3,Gg2g4] Three pawns for the piece plus we have h3-g4 ideas.}) 14. Rf1 Be6 {[%csl Be6] Only move.} 15. Ne2 $1 {[%cal Ge2g3,Gg3h5] with the idea Ng3-Nh5.} (15. e5 {[%cal Gc3e4] Looks logical, with the idea Ne4, but Black has a strong defensive idea:} Ng4 $1 {[%csl Bg4]} 16. Bxd8 Be3 17. Bxc7 Bxd2+ 18. Kxd2 Bxc4 19. dxc4 Nxe5 20. b3 $36 {[%cal Gc3d5] Nd5 next. We have more than full compensation for the exchange.}) 15... Be5 16. Ng3 Kh8 $3 {Only move.} 17. d4 $1 (17. Nh5 $2 {[%csl Rh5]} Nxe4 $19 {[%csl Be4] This is Black's idea.}) 17... Nxe4 18. Nxe4 f6 19. Bxe6 fxg5 20. Rxf8+ Qxf8 21. dxe5 Qf1+ 22. Qd1 Qf4+ 23. Nd2 Qxe5 24. Bc4 $14 {Plus versus plus. An interesting position where White has some initiative because the two pieces can potentially create an attack.} *

