{[%evp 0,14,14,11,11,10,11,11,11,-1,4,-26,-29,-29,-36,-12,2]} 1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. d3 Bc5 5. f4 d6 6. Nf3 Bg4 {Black's most common continuation, by far.} (6... Be6 $6 {[%csl Bc4] Understandably, Black wants to exchange the bishops, but this move is inaccurate because of} 7. Bb5 $1 {[%csl Rc6,Re5]} Bd7 (7... O-O 8. f5 Bd7 9. Bg5 $16 {Positional advantage. We have a better version of the 5.f4 O-O line, analyzed in a previous chapter.}) (7... a6 8. Bxc6+ bxc6 9. f5 Bd7 10. Qe2 $16 {[%cal Gc1e3] Be3 next, and positional advantage. O-O-O next, followed by the g4-g5 plan.}) 8. Na4 $1 {[%csl Ba4] Key move for the advantage.} Nd4 {Most common continuation.} (8... exf4 9. Nxc5 dxc5 10. Bxf4 $16 {Positional advantage.}) (8... Bb6 9. Nxb6 axb6 10. Bxc6 Bxc6 11. fxe5 $16 {[%csl Be5] Full extra pawn.}) 9. Nxc5 dxc5 (9... Bxb5 $2 10. Nxb7 Qb8 11. Na5 $16) 10. Nxd4 exd4 (10... cxd4 11. Bc4 $16 {[%csl Oc1,Oc4][%cal Ge1g1] Advantage thanks to the f-file and pair of bishops.}) 11. Bxd7+ Nxd7 12. O-O $16 {Positional advantage, as occurred in the legendary game Spassky-Haik, 1983.}) (6... Ng4 $2 {Concrete but wrong because of} 7. Ng5 $1 {[%csl Bg5]} h6 (7... exf4 8. Bxf7+ Kf8 9. Bxf4 Nf2 10. Qf3 Nxh1 11. Bh5 $18 {[%csl Rf8]}) (7... O-O 8. f5 $1 Bf2+ 9. Kf1 Ne3+ 10. Bxe3 Bxe3 11. h4 $16 {[%cal Gd1g4,Gd1h5]}) (7... Bf2+ 8. Kf1 exf4 9. Bxf7+ Kf8 {Nepo-Anand, 2020.} 10. Be6 $1 $18) 8. f5 $1 hxg5 9. Qxg4 f6 10. h4 $1 Nd4 {Ohanyan-Gukesh, 2023.} 11. Rh3 $3 $18 {[%cal Gh4g5] hxg5 next.}) (6... h6 {[%cal Rf3g5] Looks like a useful move against Ng5 (or Bg5) ideas, but we achieve some advantage thanks to another idea:} 7. Na4 $1 {[%csl Ba4,Rc5][%cal Ga4c5] Once we exchange the bishops, we will be able to castle and have an edge.} Bb6 (7... Bb4+ 8. c3 Ba5 9. b4 Bb6 10. Nxb6 axb6 11. O-O $16 {[%cal Gf4f5] Mekhitarian-Esipenko, 2020.}) (7... exf4 8. Nxc5 dxc5 9. Bxf4 $14 {[%cal Ge1g1]}) 8. Nxb6 axb6 9. O-O $14 {[%cal Gf4f5,Gf4e5] White has a very pleasant game.}) (6... Na5 $5 {Principled move.} 7. Bb3 Nxb3 8. axb3 a6 {[%cal Gc5a7] Securing the bishop.} (8... O-O 9. Na4 $1 $14) 9. Qe2 Bg4 10. Be3 $14 {More pleasant for White.}) (6... a6 {Second most common move.} 7. Nd5 $1 {The most interesting direction.} (7. f5 $6 b5 8. Bb3 Na5 $36 {[%csl Rb3][%cal Gc8b7,Gd6d5]}) 7... Nxd5 (7... Bg4 8. c3 $1 {[%cal Rc6d4] Stopping Nd4.} Ba7 {Probably the best, but we have a nice game after} (8... O-O 9. f5 $16 {[%cal Gc1g5,Gh2h3]}) 9. f5 $36 {[%cal Gc1g5]}) (7... h6 8. f5 Na5 {Looks critical.} (8... Ba7 9. Bd2 $1 $36 {[%cal Rc6a5,Gd1e2,Ge1c1] We stop Na5, and can continue with Qe2-O-O-O.}) 9. b4 $1 {Key move.} Nxd5 10. bxc5 Nf6 11. Bb3 (11. Bd5 $5 $14 {Also deserves attention.}) 11... dxc5 12. Nxe5 Nxb3 13. axb3 {Now Black must find the only move} Bxf5 $1 (13... Qd4 $2 14. Bf4 $18) 14. Bb2 $1 {[%cal Rd8d4]} (14. exf5 Qd4 $17 {[%cal Rc1f4]}) 14... Be6 15. O-O $36 {[%csl Bb2][%cal Gb2h8] Initiative thanks to the strong light-squared bishop.}) 8. Bxd5 Be6 $1 {Black's best.} (8... O-O $2 9. f5 $1 $16 {[%cal Gc1g5]}) (8... exf4 9. d4 $1 Bb6 (9... Nxd4 10. Nxd4 Qh4+ 11. Kf1 $14) 10. Bxf4 Be6 11. O-O O-O 12. Kh1 $14 {White had a pleasant initiative in Wei-Vallejo, 2014.}) (8... Qe7 9. f5 $1 $36) 9. Bxc6+ $5 bxc6 10. fxe5 dxe5 11. Qe2 $132 {[%cal Gc1e3] Be3 next. The position is equal, but very playable.}) (6... a5 $5 {Only eleven games, but this is trickier than 6...a6. GM Sadhwani likes to play it.} 7. f5 {[%csl Bf5][%cal Rb7b5] Compared to the 6...a6 line, here Black doesn't have the b5 idea.} (7. Nd5 $2 {Now it's a mistake!} Nxd5 8. Bxd5 exf4 9. d4 Nxd4 $1 10. Nxd4 Qh4+ 11. Kf1 f3 $1 12. gxf3 c6 13. Bb3 {[%csl Ba5] White is much better when Black's pawn is on a6, but here} a4 $1 {[%csl Ba4]} 14. Bc4 b5 $19 {[%csl Rc4]}) 7... Nd4 {Engine's continuation, and also this is how Sadhwani plays it.} 8. a4 $1 {[%cal Rb7b5] Important move, securing our bishop.} c6 9. Nxd4 $1 $146 {Best chance, because Black now has to understand how to recapture.} (9. Bg5 $2 h6 10. Bh4 {Galchenko-Sadhwani, 2022.} d5 $1 $17) 9... exd4 $1 {Correct recapture, but difficult to understand if Black is not prepared.} (9... Bxd4 $2 {Natural but wrong.} 10. Qf3 $16 {[%cal Rd6d5,Gg2g4] g4 next, with an advantage.}) 10. Ne2 d5 11. exd5 cxd5 12. Bb5+ Bd7 13. Bxd7+ Nxd7 14. O-O O-O 15. Nf4 $132 {Equal but playable.}) 7. Na4 $1 {[%csl Ba4] Important idea to fight for the advantage.} (7. h3 $2 {Is also popular, but wrong for concrete reasons:} Bxf3 8. Qxf3 Nd4 9. Qg3 (9. Qd1 d5 $1 $17) 9... O-O $15) 7... Bxf3 {Concrete line.} (7... Qe7 $6 {Positionally wrong.} 8. Nxc5 dxc5 9. Bb5 $1 $16) (7... Nd4 $2 {Mistake, but you should know the refutation.} 8. Nxc5 dxc5 9. c3 $1 {[%csl Bc3] Only move for the advantage.} Nxf3+ 10. gxf3 Bh5 (10... Be6 11. Bxe6 fxe6 12. fxe5 Nh5 13. Qe2 O-O 14. Rg1 {[%cal Gc1g5]} Nf4 15. Bxf4 Rxf4 16. O-O-O $16 {[%cal Gd3d4] d4 next and clear extra pawn.}) 11. fxe5 Nxe4 {And now it's important to know} (11... Nd7 12. Bf4 Qe7 13. Rg1 $16) 12. O-O $1 {[%csl Bg1] The only refutation.} Ng5 13. Qe2 Ne6 14. Qg2 $16 {[%cal Gf3f4,Gf4f5] f4-f5 next.}) (7... Bb6 {Mainline.} 8. h3 $1 {Correct move order.} (8. Nxb6 $6 {This is how White played in almost all the games, but it's a wrong move order!} axb6 9. h3 Bxf3 10. Qxf3 Nd4 $1 $36 {[%cal Gb6b5] b5 next.}) 8... Bxf3 9. Qxf3 Nd4 10. Qf2 $1 Ba5+ {[%csl Ba5] The best, but very computer-like.} (10... O-O 11. fxe5 dxe5 12. c3 Ne6 13. Nxb6 axb6 14. Be3 $16 {Positional advantage.}) 11. c3 b5 12. O-O $1 bxc4 13. cxd4 exd4 {For the engine, Black's position is more or less fine (+0.30), but we have good practical chances, for example:} 14. e5 Nd7 15. exd6 O-O 16. dxc4 $14) (7... O-O {Second most common move.} 8. Nxc5 dxc5 9. O-O (9. h3 $2 {Tactical mistake, so be careful!} Bxf3 10. Qxf3 b5 $1 $17 {[%cal Gc6d4]}) (9. f5 {Also possible} Na5 10. Qe2 Nxc4 11. dxc4 $132 {Equal but playable.}) 9... Qd6 {Most common move.} (9... Nh5 10. f5 Nd4 11. a4 $14 {[%csl Bc4] Securing the bishop.}) 10. Qd2 $5 (10. f5 $5 $36 {Also interesting.}) 10... Bxf3 11. gxf3 exf4 12. Qxf4 $14 {[%csl Bc1,Bc4] We can go for a long game with the pair of bishops.}) 8. Qxf3 Nd4 9. Qd1 b5 10. Bxf7+ $1 {[%csl Bf7] Powerful piece sacrifice.} Kxf7 11. Nxc5 exf4 {Probably the best, but very difficult to play for Black over the board.} (11... dxc5 12. fxe5 Nd7 13. c3 Ne6 14. O-O+ $16 {[%cal Gd3d4] d4 next, and our powerful pawns give us an advantage.}) 12. Nb3 Ne6 13. O-O g5 14. a4 b4 15. c3 bxc3 16. bxc3 $40 {[%cal Ga1a2,Ga2f2,Gg2g3] Ra2-Raf2, then we will open the f-file and we have all the fun for free as the material is equal.} *

