nd here we can try some ideas. For example:} 13. Nd5 (13. f3 {Also logical, and Black needs to find:} Bd7 14. fxe4 Nxe4 15. Nxe4 fxe4 16. Bc4 $132 {We have a playable endgame, and it's risk free for White.}) 13... Bd7 {Only move for Black to be fine.} 14. Nxf6+ gxf6 15. Ne2 $132 {We definitely reached a playable position.}) 9. O-O-O Nc6 10. Nge2 Bg4 11. h3 {To keep the structure intact.} (11. a3 {Was tried in one game, but it allows Black to ruin our structure.} Bxc3 12. bxc3 O-O 13. h3 {Horvath - Melikhov, 2022.} Be6 $11 {It seems fine for Black.}) 11... Bxc3 12. hxg4 Be5 13. Bxe5 Nxe5 14. f3 exf3 15. gxf3 {[%csl Rb7,Bg2][%cal Gf1g2,Gg4g5,Gf3f4] Here Black should play concretely, otherwise we are just better:} Nxf3 16. Bg2 Ne5 17. Bxb7 Rb8 18. g5 Nfg4 {Black needs to maintain a normal structure.} (18... Rxb7 {Isn't a good trade for Black.} 19. gxf6 gxf6 20. Nf4 $14 {Definitely some chances here, as our structure is much better.}) 19. Bg2 Ne3 20. Rdg1 Nxg2 21. Rxg2 $132 {It's equal, but White definitely has practical chances, as it's easier to advance the pawns. The g5-pawn is very annoying for Black.}) 7. Nb5 Bb4+ 8. c3 dxc3 {Critical.} (8... Ba5 {Also fine, according to the engine.} 9. O-O-O Qe7 10. Nxd4 Nxd4 11. Qxd4 Bb6 {Only move for Black now.} 12. Qa4+ Bd7 13. Qc2 O-O-O 14. Bd3 {It seems like White is just up a pawn, but for the engine it's almost full compensation.} Ng4 15. Bg3 {No games here and with normal moves White could definitely be slightly better. But, Black can go for the concrete:} Bxf2 16. Bxf2 Ba4 {Very much in the engine style, but this is clearly compensation for Black.} 17. b3 Qa3+ 18. Kb1 Bxb3 {Another strong sacrifice, and the line continues with:} 19. axb3 Nxf2 20. Qxf2 Qxb3+ 21. Kc1 Qxc3+ 22. Qc2 Qa3+ 23. Kd2 Rxd3+ {Now Black gets the queen.} 24. Qxd3 Rd8 25. Qxd8+ Kxd8 26. Ne2 $13 {And for the engine it's of course "0.00" but over the board anything can happen.}) 9. bxc3 (9. Qxd8+ {Also possible.} Kxd8 10. Bxc7+ {bxc3 transposes into the 9.bxc3 line, but I wanted to see if we can avoid the draw:} Ke7 11. bxc3 Bc5 {White is not better, but we can try to play this. For example:} 12. e5 Ne8 {This is the correct move for Black here.} (12... Ne4 {Looks more active, but gives White chances.} 13. f3 a6 {Forced.} (13... Nf2 $2 {Simple blunder.} 14. Bd6+ $18 {White just wins a piece.}) 14. fxe4 axb5 15. Bxb5 $14 {And White is slightly better.}) 13. O-O-O {Black has many moves. For example:} Bxf2 14. Bd6+ Nxd6 15. exd6+ Kd8 16. Nc7 {We can include this to force the rook to b8.} Rb8 17. Nf3 Bd7 18. Bc4 $132 {White is not better, but also not worse, so this could be an alternative to avoid the immediate draw.}) 9... Ba5 10. Qxd8+ Kxd8 11. O-O-O+ (11. f3 {Is the same.} a6 {[%emt 0:00:00] Necessary here, and transposes into 11.0-0-0.}) 11... Bd7 12. f3 {Necessary.} a6 13. Nd4 Nxd4 14. cxd4 {Visually we are doing great, but Black can force a draw with:} Ba4 15. Rd3 Bb5 16. Rd1 Ba4 $11 {We have nothing better than the repetition, and this is the only drawback of this line. But, this draw has only been forced at the GM-level so far.}) (5... Be7 {Is also clearly not critical.} 6. Bf4 {[%csl Re7]} d6 7. O-O-O O-O 8. h3 $14 {[%cal Gg2g4] White just achieved a great position.}) (5... g6 $2 {Is just bad.} 6. Bg5 Bg7 7. f4 $16 {White already has an advantage.}) 6. Bf4 (6. Qg3 {I also checked this move, but it doesn't give White anything.} Nb4 7. Bd3 d5 {Black quickly gets strong counterplay.} 8. Nge2 O-O 9. Nxd5 Nfxd5 10. exd5 Qxd5 11. Bh6 Nxd3+ 12. cxd3 Bd4 {A convenient way to defend g7.} 13. Nxd4 Qxd4 14. O-O Qxb2 $11 {And this is simply very comfortable for Black.}) 6... d6 {Logical continuation.} (6... O-O {A less accurate move order, as it gives White the chance to play:} 7. O-O-O Ng4 (7... Bxf2 $6 {Is a mistake, due to:} 8. e5 $14 {And White is better.}) 8. Nh3 Nxf2 9. Nxf2 Bxf2 10. e5 $132 {This is very interesting to play for White. For example:} Bd4 {Is the most common move, but after:} 11. Qg3 $14 {White is probably already better.}) 7. O-O-O $5 {My recommendation is to sacrifice a pawn here.} (7. Qd2 {Defending the pawn is too slow.} Be6 8. O-O-O Bb4 $1 $36 {Is a very strong idea for Black.}) (7. f3 $2 {Looks logical, but runs into:} Nh5 $17 {[%csl Bh5][%cal Gd8h4] With huge problems for White.}) 7... Bxf2 $1 {The best for Black is to accept the challenge.} (7... Ng4 {To take on f2 with the knight.} 8. Nh3 $1 Nxf2 9. Nxf2 Bxf2 10. Qf3 Bd4 11. Bb5 {To force Black to take.} Bxc3 12. Qxc3 O-O 13. Bxc6 bxc6 14. e5 d5 15. Be3 $14 {[%csl Rc6,Be3] We have a very good bishop and a lot of potential.} Bd7 (15... Qd7 {Looks normal, but after:} 16. Rhf1 $16 {It's suddenly much better for White (+1 evaluation).}) 16. b3 $14 {[%csl Bb3][%cal Ge3d4,Gh1f1,Gh2h4,Gg2g4] We can play normal moves for now, the position is very pleasant.}) (7... O-O {Very easy for Black.} 8. f3 {We are quite happy to play this move.} Be6 9. Qd2 $1 Nd7 10. Kb1 $14 {[%cal Gg1e2,Gh2h4] Simply very comfortable for White.}) (7... Be6 {Similar to 7...O-O.} 8. f3 Qd7 9. Be3 {We exchange the bishops here.} Bxe3+ 10. Qxe3 a6 {Necessary and now Black manages O-O-O.} (10... O-O-O $6 {Fails to:} 11. e5 $16 {[%csl Ra7] Problems with a7.}) 11. h4 O-O-O 12. Nge2 $14 {[%cal Ge2f4] But now it's a normal position, and White is slightly better.}) 8. Nf3 (8. e5 $6 {Doesn't work, due to:} Nh5 $15 {Black's key idea.}) 8... O-O {The only way that Black can keep the small plus.} (8... Bb6 {Dubov - Nakamura, 2020.} 9. Bg5 {A very good option, but Dubov didn't play it.} h6 10. Bh4 g5 {Is Black's best option.} (10... O-O {Normal, but after:} 11. Be2 $132 {[%csl Rf6][%cal Gh1f1] There could be some problems with the knight on f6.}) 11. Be1 O-O 12. Kb1 $132 {[%csl Rg5] For the engine it's "0.00", but practically it's dangerous for Black.}) (8... Be6 {Also logical, and has been played once.} 9. Qe2 $1 {Here we go for the e5 idea.} Bc5 10. e5 Nd7 11. exd6 {We get the pawn back.} Bxd6 12. Bxd6 cxd6 13. Rxd6 Qe7 14. Qd2 {It's not a big advantage for White, but it's playable. For example:} O-O-O (14... O-O {Runs into:} 15. Bc4 $14 {[%csl Rd7] And Black has problems with d7.}) 15. Qf4 Nc5 16. Rxd8+ Rxd8 17. Bb5 $132 {A playable position, and we don't have any flaws now.}) (8... Bg4 {Has also been played in some games, but definitely gives White a good game.} 9. h3 Bxf3 10. Qxf3 Bd4 11. Bb5 $1 {Forcing Black to take on c3.} Bxc3 12. Qxc3 O-O 13. Rhe1 {White has a pleasant position. For example:} Nh5 14. Bxc6 Nxf4 15. Bxb7 Rb8 16. Ba6 $14 {This can potentially be better for White, as the bishop could easily be stronger than the knight.}) 9. Qe2 Bc5 {The best move, and here Black keeps the beloved "minus" evaluation.} (9... Bb6 {Also possible.} 10. Bg5 {We go for the same critical line as against Bc5.} h6 11. Bh4 Bg4 12. h3 Nd4 13. Qd3 Bxf3 14. gxf3 Ne6 {And in the same position with the bishop on c5 we play e5, but here we can play:} 15. Nd5 {To take on b6.} Ng5 16. Nxb6 axb6 17. Kb1 $132 {[%csl Bf1,Bh4][%cal Gh1g1] "0.00" for the engine, but it's much easier for White with the pair of bishops. I certainly wouldn't be happy here with Black.}) (9... Nh5 $2 {Played by GM Fridman, but it's a serious mistake.} 10. Bxd6 $1 cxd6 11. Qxf2 $16 {White was better in Amar - Fridman, 2022.}) 10. Bg5 h6 {Necessary.} (10... Bg4 $6 {Was played in the only game that reached this position, but after:} 11. Nd5 $14 {It's a nightmare for Black.}) 11. Bh4 {Here Black only has two moves in order not to lose:} Bg4 {To prepare Nd4.} (11... Re8 {Also possible, but extremely difficult to understand.} 12. Nd5 g5 13. Nxg5 Nxd5 {Necessary.} 14. Rxd5 Qf6 {Only move, but it's very much in the engine style.} 15. Qd2 Nb4 16. Nf3 Qg6 17. Rxc5 {Important move.} dxc5 18. a3 {It's very easy to go wrong with Black.} a5 $1 {[%cal Rf1d3] It's important not to allow Bd3, and White of course doesn't want to allow Black to have the open a-file.} (18... Nc6 {Logical to keep the piece, but after:} 19. Bd3 $16 {[%cal Ge4e5] With e5 next, Black is in serious trouble.} Ne5 $2 {Simply loses.} 20. Nxe5 Rxe5 21. Rf1 $18 {Rf3-Rg3 ideas, and it's game over for Black.}) 19. e5 Bf5 20. Bf6 Nxc2 {This is a forced line:} 21. Bc4 Ne3 $1 22. Be2 Bb1 $1 23. Ne1 {Now the game can end in an amusing repetition.} Bf5 (23... c4 $5 {Black can also keep the game going.} 24. Qxe3 Qxf6 25. Kxb1 Rxe5 26. Qg3+ {We can just repeat.} Rg5 27. Qe3 Re5 28. Qg3+ $13 {Black can repeat, or keep the game going, but it will also be risky for him to play on.}) 24. Nf3 {[%cal Gf3h4]} Bb1 $11 {But, of course finding all these moves over the board is extremely difficult.}) 12. h3 Nd4 13. Qd3 Bxf3 14. gxf3 Ne6 15. e5 (15. Nd5 {This was our move against the 9...Bb6 line, but here after:} Ng5 $15 {[%csl Bc5][%cal Rd5b6] We simply don't have Nxb6.}) 15... dxe5 {Black is forced to take.} 16. Qf5 Be3+ 17. Kb1 Nd4 {Only move.} 18. Qxe5 Re8 {Another only move for Black.} 19. Qh2 c6 {This is the only variation where Black keeps the small minus.} 20. Ne4 g5 {Forced.} 21. Bg3 {Black can play Nxe4 or Qe7, and it's only slightly worse for him, but only:} Nd5 {Gives Black the minus evaluation.} 22. Bd3 Qb6 23. Ka1 $13 {[%cal Gh3h4] According to the engine, this is the best that Black can achieve in this line. We are very deep in a new line, and the position clearly has possibilities for three results. We have a lot of ideas here and we are waiting for a prepared opponent to walk into this, to see how he will handle this situation.} *

