{[%evp 0,18,20,18,13,23,14,21,11,-2,21,-17,61,28,45,26,31,38,34,33,34]} 1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 {In this chapter, we are going to look at the sidelines.} Nc6 (2... d6 {Also possible, and we have two good continuations:} 3. Nf3 (3. d4 {is also fine.} exd4 4. Qxd4 Nc6 {[%csl Od6] We get an improved version of the 2...Nf6 line, as Black has committed to d6.} 5. Qd3 Nf6 6. Bf4 $14 {We analyzed this line in a previous chapter, where d5 or Bc5 were the critical moves. Here they are not possible, so it's great for us.}) 3... Nf6 4. d4 {Transposing into a Philidor.}) 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. d3 Be7 {Probably the best sideline for Black.} (4... d6 {Here we simply play:} 5. f4 Be7 (5... Bg4 {is the most common now.} 6. Nge2 {But it helps us with development, and Black probably doesn't want to simply give up the bishop.}) 6. Nf3 Bg4 (6... O-O {is also possible.} 7. f5 $14 {We can play in the spirit of the Bc5 line here.}) 7. h3 {to force a decision from Black.} Bxf3 8. Qxf3 Nd4 9. Qf2 c6 10. a4 O-O 11. O-O $36 {We have a very comfortable position to play.}) (4... h6 {Played in approximately one thousand games, but after:} 5. f4 $36 {[%csl Bf4,Rh6] We have a better version of everything that we have analyzed so far.}) 5. a3 {[%csl Ba3,Yc4] I prefer to secure the bishop now.} (5. f4 {Black hasn't played d6 yet, so d5 is an option.} exf4 6. Bxf4 O-O 7. Nf3 Na5 {to take the bishop.} 8. Bb3 Nxb3 9. axb3 d5 $132 {And it's absolutely fine for Black.}) 5... O-O 6. Nge2 d6 7. O-O {[%cal Gf2f4] Only now do we think about f4.} Be6 8. Nd5 Nb8 {was tried by Grandmaster Postny.} (8... Bxd5 {Most common move here.} 9. exd5 Nb8 10. Ng3 $14 {[%cal Gf2f4,Gg3f5] And our opening clearly worked out well. We have a new and interesting position, and it's even slightly better.}) 9. Ng3 (9. a4 {is also a good option.} c6 10. Nxe7+ Qxe7 11. Ng3 $14 {With a slightly better position for White.}) 9... Nxd5 10. exd5 Bc8 {Nabaty - Postny, 2019.} 11. d4 $14 {Nabaty played f4, but I think that White is slightly better, so clearly the opening was successful.} *

