{[%tqu "En","Sadhwani line. White to move.","","","f4f5","Black doesn't have the b5 idea.",10,"c3d5","Don't confuse with 6...a6, here it's a mistake!",0]} 7.f5 $1 {[%csl Bf5][%cal Rb7b5] Black doesn't have the b5 idea.} (7.Nd5 $2 {Don't confuse with 6...a6; here it's a mistake!} Nxd5 8.Bxd5 exf4 9.d4 Nxd4 $1 10.Nxd4 Qh4+ 11.Kf1 f3 $1 12.gxf3 c6 13.Bb3 {[%csl Ba5] White is much better when Black's pawn is on a6, but here} a4 $1 {[%csl Ba4]} 14.Bc4 b5 $19 {[%csl Rc4] The bishop is trapped.}) 7...Nd4 {The engine's continuation, and also this is how Sadhwani plays it.} 8.a4 $1 {[%csl Ga4][%cal Rb7b5] An important move, securing our bishop.} c6 9.Nxd4 $1 $146 {The best chance, because Black now has to understand how to recapture.} (9.Bg5 $2 {Too naive.} h6 10.Bh4 {Galchenko-Sadhwani, 2022.} d5 $1 $17 {[%csl Bd5] Black is much better.}) 9...exd4 $1 {The correct recapture, but difficult to understand if Black is not prepared.} 10.Ne2 $132 {[%cal Gd6d5] Black has to push d5, but after the capture and Bb5+, it's an interesting and playable position.} *

